                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" notBefore="1100" notAfter="1149">
                                s.xii¹</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_122">Canterbury,
                                Kent, Benedictine abbey of St Augustine (originally of St Peter and
                                St Paul)</orgName>
                            <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval
                                Libraries of Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the
                                Supplement (1987). There are two corresponding Medieval Libraries of
                                Great Britain index cards. One of these cards was originally written
                                by Richard Hunt, and it records the following information: <q>At the
                                    top of fol. 1 are traces of what appears to be the press-mark of
                                    St. Augustine's Canterbury.</q> However, Neil Ker later added
                                the word <q>reject</q> along with the following note: <q>I can see
                                    only what looks like 'd', perhaps a price mark.</q> The second
                                index card (a duplicate) shows a similar evolution of
                                ideas.</p><p>Compare also the rejected entry for this manuscript in
                                B. C. Barker-Benfield (ed.), <title>St. Augustine's Abbey,
                                    Canterbury</title>, CMBLC 13, 3 vols (London, 2008), vol. 3, p.
                                1828: <q>Damaged by mould. Warner &amp; Gilson, I. 61 reported
                                        <q>traces of what appears to be the press-mark of St
                                        Augustine's, Canterbury</q> at the top of fol. 1, but
                                    nothing of this is now visible. The sec.-fol. reading is
                                        <q>sepulturae suae</q> (fol. 2r, prol.) or <q>uera</q> (fol.
                                    3r, main text, same word in both text and gloss): neither of
                                    these finds any echo at BA 1.177-</q>.</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo">John
                                Theyer</persName>. (CMA 6465 Joh. Evangelium
                            glossatum).</provenance>
                    </history>
